<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SR-lib.props — the MSBuild counterpart of CMakeLists.txt's
  srlib_apply_delayload(). Import it from any .vcxproj that links SR-lib (or the
  SR import libraries directly):

      <Import Project="$(VCTargetsPath)\Microsoft.Cpp.props" />
      <Import Project="path\to\SR-lib\SR-lib.props" />

  and delete whatever hand-written <DelayLoadDLLs> list the project was
  carrying. Both platforms are handled; nothing else about the project changes.

  WHY THIS EXISTS

  Linking the SR import libraries writes hard imports for the SR runtime DLLs
  into the consuming module's import table. The Windows loader resolves those at
  process start, so on a machine that has never had the SR Platform installed
  the module fails to load — for an EXE that is a modal popup naming a DLL,
  fired before main() runs; for an injected DLL it is a load failure the host
  reports as "the mod doesn't work". /DELAYLOAD makes the loader skip them until
  first call, which is what lets SR.cpp's LoadLibrary probes report a missing
  runtime as E_NOINTERFACE instead of the process dying.

  Delay-load cannot be baked into a static library, so it has to be applied to
  the FINAL module (EXE/DLL) — which means every consumer used to hand-copy this
  list, and silently regressed whenever SR.cpp started referencing a new SR
  component. That is not hypothetical: SimulatedRealityDisplays (lens hint) and
  SimulatedRealityFaceTrackers (head/eye tracking) entered the import table when
  those APIs were added, and a consumer that didn't notice shipped a binary that
  refused to load without the runtime. CMake consumers are covered by
  srlib_apply_delayload(); this file closes the same hole for MSBuild.

  opencv_world343.dll is THE one people miss: no consumer writes OpenCV code and
  OpenCV appears nowhere in the project, but the SR import libs inline
  cv::String destructors, so the linker emits a real import for it anyway. It is
  usually the DLL the loader popup names.

  The list is deliberately complete rather than tailored per consumer —
  /DELAYLOAD on a DLL you don't import is a no-op. It costs one LNK4199 per
  unused entry, which is why /ignore:4199 is set below; without that the noise
  trains people to ignore link warnings.

  Keep in sync with SRLIB_DELAYLOAD_DLLS / SRLIB_DELAYLOAD_DLLS_32 in
  CMakeLists.txt.

  VERIFY (do this before shipping, on both platforms):
      dumpbin /imports MyModule.dll | findstr ".dll"
  Every SR and OpenCV DLL must appear ONLY under "delay load imports". Anything
  SR-related in the top-level import section will not load on a clean machine.
-->
<Project ToolsVersion="4.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">

  <!-- 64-bit: unsuffixed SR DLL names. "simulatedreality" is the aggregate
       library and exists only here — there is no 32-bit twin. -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Platform)'=='x64'">
    <SRLibDelayLoadDLLs>simulatedreality.dll;SimulatedRealityCore.dll;SimulatedRealityCameras.dll;SimulatedRealityHandTrackers.dll;SimulatedRealityFaceTrackers.dll;SimulatedRealityUserModelers.dll;SimulatedRealityDisplays.dll;SimulatedRealityDirectX.dll;SimulatedRealityOpenGL.dll;DimencoWeaving.dll;opencv_world343.dll</SRLibDelayLoadDLLs>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- 32-bit: the SR DLLs carry a "32" suffix; the third-party ones do not. -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Platform)'=='Win32'">
    <SRLibDelayLoadDLLs>SimulatedRealityCore32.dll;SimulatedRealityCameras32.dll;SimulatedRealityHandTrackers32.dll;SimulatedRealityFaceTrackers32.dll;SimulatedRealityUserModelers32.dll;SimulatedRealityDisplays32.dll;SimulatedRealityDirectX32.dll;SimulatedRealityOpenGL32.dll;DimencoWeaving32.dll;opencv_world343.dll</SRLibDelayLoadDLLs>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemDefinitionGroup Condition="'$(SRLibDelayLoadDLLs)'!=''">
    <Link>
      <DelayLoadDLLs>$(SRLibDelayLoadDLLs);%(DelayLoadDLLs)</DelayLoadDLLs>
      <!-- delayimp.lib provides the delay-load helper the /DELAYLOAD thunks
           call. MSBuild usually adds it implicitly; naming it is harmless and
           keeps the props self-contained for hand-rolled link lines. -->
      <AdditionalDependencies>delayimp.lib;%(AdditionalDependencies)</AdditionalDependencies>
      <!-- LNK4199: "/DELAYLOAD:x.dll ignored; no imports found" — expected for
           every entry this particular consumer doesn't touch. -->
      <AdditionalOptions>/ignore:4199 %(AdditionalOptions)</AdditionalOptions>
    </Link>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>

</Project>
